-**Parameters and Typical Values:**
-*   $N$: Number of tissue types. In the medical imaging study, $N$ was sufficient to define air-skin and tissue-bone interfaces [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   $fv_n$: Known CT numbers. In the molecular graphics study, these corresponded to electron density values for atoms [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   $\alpha_{vn}$: Target opacities. These are user-defined or derived from tissue properties [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+**Parameters and Typical Values:** $N$ is the number of tissue types, sufficient to define air-skin and tissue-bone interfaces in the medical study [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. $fv_n$ are known CT numbers, corresponding to electron density values for atoms in the molecular graphics study [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. $\alpha_{vn}$ are target opacities, user-defined or derived from tissue properties [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
 
-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Adjacency Violation:** If tissue types do not touch only adjacent types in the ordered CT number list, voxels at boundaries cannot be unambiguously classified, leading to incorrect rendering [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Thin Features:** Binary thresholds may miss thin features of a tissue type if voxel values fall below the threshold. This method avoids that by mapping all voxels to non-zero opacity (within the defined range), rendering thin regions as faint wisps [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Gradient Constancy:** The thickness of the transition region around isosurface contours remains constant only if the local gradient magnitude is constant within a radius of $r$ voxels around each point on the surface [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Anisotropy and Motion:** CT scanners have anisotropic spatial sensitivity, causing striping artifacts. Patient motion also causes striping because shading depends on gradient orientation, and misalignments between adjacent slices disrupt this [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Non-Adjacency Handling:** For datasets not meeting adjacency criteria (e.g., internal soft tissue organs), users can interactively clip or carve away unwanted tissues to isolate subsets that meet the criteria [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
